  "query_preview": "..\nThe large scale pattern has a baggy trough to our west \nand high pressure aloft centered along the far eastern Texas Gulf \nCoast. A storm system will move across the northern tier of states \nFriday and Saturday, flattening the upper high and shifting it over \nthe Gulf. A low pressure system and associated trough will then move \nacross central California and the Great Basin early next week as \nHurricane Polo moves northwestward to along the Baja Mexico coast.\n\nThe showers and thunderstorms earlier today across central/western \nPima County were in response to a mid level disturbance now parked \nover far southwest Arizona. The models lift this disturbance slowly \nnorth/northeast across Arizona later today. PWATs hold steady in the \n1.0 to 1.3 inch range today, with isolated to scattered showers and \nthunderstorms developing during the afternoon hours mainly east of a \nNogales to Sells line. The high res models then lift these showers \nand thunderstorms to the northeast across the eastern portion of the \nforecast area through the evening hours tonight. The stronger \nthunderstorms will be capable of producing heavy rainfall and an \nisolated threat of flash flooding.  \n\nDrier air begins to overspread southeast Arizona from the west on \nFriday, as the flow turns southwest/west in response to the system \ntracking east across the northern tier of states. There will still \nbe a slight chance of showers and thunderstorms on Friday across far \neastern areas along the New Mexico state line. However by Saturday, \nPWATs lower down into the 0.60 to 0.80 inch range and shut down \nconvection chances across southeast Arizona. \n  \nSunday into early next week, the combination of an upper low/trough \nmoving into the West Coast and Hurricane Polo moving northward along \nthe Mexico coast will help spread moisture back across southeast \nArizona from the south. This moisture begins to move back into the \narea on Sunday, with deeper moisture moving in Monday and Tuesday \nand a chance of showers and thunderstorms across all of southeast \nArizona. The NHC official track for Hurricane Polo now shows Polo \nmoving west of the Baja Peninsula tip late Sunday and then curving \neastward into central Gulf of California south of the Baja Spur by 6 \nAM Tuesday. While Polo will help push moisture northward through \nMexico as it tracks north, this track/timing will have little direct \nimpact on southeast Arizona as most ensembles continue to curve Polo \neastward into central Sonora to the south of Hermosillo. The current \nNHC track/timing would put Polo on the back side of the trough axis \nswinging through the region, which would then shunt Polo remnants \nnortheast across northern Mexico and into New Mexico and west Texas. \nWhile there is still some uncertainty in the exact track/timing of \nPolo, what may be a bigger player in our weather early next week \n(than the Polo remnants) will be sharp trough dynamics interacting \nwith the moisture in place across southeast Arizona as this system \nmoves across the region. \n\nBehind the trough, there will be a slight chance of showers and \nthunderstorms along the International Border/New Mexico state line \non Wednesday of next week. High pressure aloft then builds over the \neastern Pacific Ocean and southeast Arizona dries out significantly \nunder westerly flow for the end of next week. Both the calendar date \n(October) and moisture profile reflect the end of Monsoon 2026. \n\nOverall, high temperatures will be a couple of degrees below normal \ntoday, warming to slightly above normal over the weekend and then \nlowering several degrees below normal early to mid next week.",
